{"tool_name":"eaglec","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"eaglec","home":"https://github.com/XiaoTaoWang/EagleC","license":"Other","summary":"A deep-learning framework for predicting a full range of structural variations from bulk and single-cell contact maps","addition_date":"2022-09-13T17:22:29.235650Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T15:39:50.890783Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Workbench"],"accessibility":"Restricted access","biotools_curie":"biotools:eaglec","cost":"Free of charge (with restrictions)","credit":[{"email":"yue@northwestern.edu","name":"Feng Yue","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7954-5462","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Xiaotao Wang"},{"name":"Yu Luan"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2429"},{"term":"Residue contact prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0272"},{"term":"Structural variation detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3228"}]}],"name":"EagleC","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"Chan019","publication":[{"doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abn9215","metadata":{"abstract":"The Hi-C technique has been shown to be a promising method to detect structural variations (SVs) in human genomes. However, algorithms that can use Hi-C data for a full-range SV detection have been severely lacking. Current methods can only identify interchromosomal translocations and long-range intrachromosomal SVs (>1 Mb) at less-than-optimal resolution. Therefore, we develop EagleC, a framework that combines deep-learning and ensemble-learning strategies to predict a full range of SVs at high resolution. We show that EagleC can uniquely capture a set of fusion genes that are missed by whole-genome sequencing or nanopore. Furthermore, EagleC also effectively captures SVs in other chromatin interaction platforms, such as HiChIP, Chromatin interaction analysis with paired-end tag sequencing (ChIA-PET), and capture Hi-C. We apply EagleC in more than 100 cancer cell lines and primary tumors and identify a valuable set of high-quality SVs. Last, we demonstrate that EagleC can be applied to single-cell Hi-C and used to study the SV heterogeneity in primary tumors.","authors":[{"name":"Luan Y."},{"name":"Wang X."},{"name":"Yue F."}],"citationCount":40,"date":"2022-06-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Science Advances","title":"EagleC: A deep-learning framework for detecting a full range of structural variations from bulk and single-cell contact maps"},"pmcid":"PMC9200291","pmid":"35704579"}],"topic":[{"term":"Gene structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0114"},{"term":"Machine learning","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3474"},{"term":"RNA-Seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3170"},{"term":"Structural variation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3175"},{"term":"Whole genome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3673"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"EagleC","home":"https://bio.tools/eaglec","license":"Other","summary":"A deep-learning framework for predicting a full range of structural variations from bulk and single-cell contact maps","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:eaglec/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":true,"registration":false},"packages":true,"source_packages":true,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":true,"license":true,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":false,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":71,"citations":[{"count":4,"year":"2022"},{"count":13,"year":"2023"},{"count":13,"year":"2026"},{"count":16,"year":"2025"},{"count":25,"year":"2024"}],"doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abn9215","pmcid":"PMC9200291","pmid